[#:seon.task{:name "class-prune-deletes-a-directory-a-check-is-sharing-before-its-jvm-launches"
             :kind :function
             :intent "prune-program-classes! spares only class directories named on a live process's classpath, so a directory a check has selected and is sharing (its throwaway JVM not launched yet) is deletable by a concurrent launch's prune. Observed 2026-10-08 with two other check JVMs running: bin/seon check failed in its share phase (share-ms 11064) with NoSuchFileException on target/dev-program-classes/ce2aa921.../seon/test$tally$iter__19767__19771.class; the identical retry passed. Diagnostic: data/clusters/default/logs/operator-check-20261008T160343.511.edn. Done when a directory selected by a pending launch is held from selection to exit, with a regression that prunes concurrently with a share."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/prune-program-classes!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-deep-check-queue-times-out-waiting-for-a-slot"
             :kind :function
             :intent "With 10 bin/seon check --test runs queued for the 2 throwaway-JVM slots, the last answered 'No throwaway JVM slot freed within its bound' after waiting: the slot wait is capped at the boot bound, not at the queue's expected drain (each check ~50-100 s). Lanes then retry blind. Done when a queued check waits proportionally to the queue ahead of it (or reports its position and expected wait) instead of failing as if a slot were stuck."}
 #:seon.task{:name "check-cannot-run-tests-of-host-bound-namespaces-against-a-candidate"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Host-bound namespaces (render/web and others compiled into the JVM) cannot be redefined on a branch, so their reaching tests cannot prove a candidate edit. Done when bin/seon check runs the reaching tests against the candidate on its throwaway JVM for a change in a host-bound namespace, with a landing that proves it on a real render/web change."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/check!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/repl-audit--change.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "check-test-cannot-run-platform-tests"
             :kind :function
             :intent "bin/seon check --test intermittently failed on the throwaway JVM with 'branch :agent-… is absent from the store roster' (`seon.cluster.store/open-branch!`). Cause (verified 2026-10-06): konserve's tiered store under Datahike's overlay (:frontend-only writes; seon.test clean-request-handle uses `seon.cluster.store/open-overlay!`): a cold :frontend-first read warmed the memory frontend fire-and-forget, the :branches update after it (datahike versioning.cljc:178) read the still-empty frontend and wrote only the new branch, and the late warm replaced that write with the store's roster. Reproduced on an overlay: k/update :branches with an uncached roster gave #{:agent-y}, dropping :db. Fixed in the konserve fork 8700ca7 (warm only an absent key; a :frontend-only update seeds from the backend), pinned in deps.edn; regression `seon.cluster.store-test/an-overlay-roster-write-keeps-the-stores-branches`. Done when the pin is loaded (JVM restart), repeated concurrent checks never refuse this way, and cold-start's boot-failure assertion runs green. Also seen: two concurrent checks of the SAME sha race in committed-source! ('staging-<pid> -> data/source/<sha>: Directory not empty')."}
 #:seon.task{:name "check-test-reports-failed-for-an-excluded-long-test"
             :kind :function
             :intent "bin/seon check --test NS reports passed? false with zero failures for a namespace that holds an excluded declared-long test (seon.test.clj:739 excludes long tests unless the request sets :seon.test/include-long? true), so bin/lane commit --test refuses a green slice. Done when bin/seon check and bin/lane accept --include-long and pass it through to the test request (script/seon/operator.clj check!, test-check-arguments, bin/lane), an excluded long test with no failure reports passed, and a regression covers both. Reconfirmed 2026-10-10 (loop-prove, 86b7cdbbe): `--test NS` answers passed? false with 0 failures while a declared-long test of NS is excluded, so lanes must name every var of the namespace; the fix above (an excluded test with no failure reports passed, or the answer names the excluded vars) removes that need."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/check! seon.test/run}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/commit-floor--report.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "committed-source-same-sha-race"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Two bin/seon check runs of the SAME commit, started together while data/source/<sha> does not yet exist, race in committed-source! (script/seon/operator.clj:912-943): each builds data/source/<sha>.staging-<pid> and the loser's (fs/move staging target {:atomic-move true}) refuses with '…/<sha>.staging-<pid> -> …/data/source/<sha>: Directory not empty', failing the whole check before it rehearses (2 of 4 concurrent checks of 28ab7df1f, 2026-10-06 08:21, diagnostics data/clusters/default/logs/operator-check-20261006T082157.679.edn and .680.edn). The loser's build is complete and identical by construction (the archive is a function of the sha), so losing the rename is success: answer the winner's target and delete the staging. Done when concurrent checks of one not-yet-built sha all proceed, with a regression that runs two committed-source! of one fresh sha at once."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/committed-source!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "status-prints-ok-while-the-cluster-is-still-starting"
             :kind :function
             :intent "bin/seon status prints OK while the cluster is still starting. Done when status prints the boot layer reached and a non-OK state until readiness is published (`seon.cluster.boot/readiness`), with a test on a booting fixture. Boot readiness layers are also not logged anywhere persistent (start output is the only record): record them as facts."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.boot/readiness}}
 #:seon.task{:name "operator-prints-whole-results"
             :kind :namespace
             :intent "The operator and dev scripts print whole result values with prn outside the value printer (seon.operator -main's :else prn of the result and its headline's MISSING collection, harness_costs measure, seon.schema.admission -main): an unknown option printed the whole nested error map twice (observed 2026-10-07). Inventory class cli-print, 4 sites: script/operator.clj 2, script/dev/harness_costs.clj 1, schema/admission.clj 1. Done when the operator prints its outcome through the value printer's budget (headline plus the diagnostic file path), and script/seon/operator_outcome_test asserts an argv failure prints one bounded line."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/check!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/no-print-hash--print-sites.tsv" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/no-print-hash--notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "check-rehearsal-cost-follows-drift-not-the-change"
             :kind :function
             :intent "bin/seon check rehearses a FULL refresh against the program the running JVM published (`seon.cluster/rehearse-source!` passes `:seon.source/changed-paths` []), so its cost follows how far HEAD has drifted from the running JVM's commit, not the candidate's change: on 2026-10-07 default ran a8e3bde5b, 15 commits and 89 program files behind HEAD 433214bc5, and `bin/seon check` of HEAD itself took 55.4 s (published 45.5 s, adopted 9.5 s, 2,008 identities). Every lane commit paid it (a docs commit 42.6 s of 56 s, a task-file commit 16.5 s of 21 s, at smaller drift). Done when a check's rehearsal cost follows the candidate's delta from HEAD (e.g. HEAD's rehearsal publication kept once per commit and the candidate's changed paths published partially on it), measured: a one-file check under 5 s with default 15 commits behind."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/check! seon.cluster/rehearse-source!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/lane-tool/notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "check-decides-documentation-by-input-roots"
             :kind :function
             :intent "bin/seon check publishes and adopts a candidate whose paths are all outside `seon.test.cache/input-roots` (documentation), contrary to 'documentation never publishes or widens gates'. bin/lane now skips the check for a commit whose every path is under docs/ and that names no --test, an interim rule beside the declared roots because the operator (babashka) cannot load seon.test.cache (it requires hasch, which babashka cannot load) and script/seon/operator.clj was held by another lane. Done when `seon.operator/check!` classifies the paths by the declared input roots, answers a no-input candidate without rehearsal, and bin/lane's documentation_only is deleted."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/check! seon.test.cache/input-roots}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/lane-tool/notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "check-rehearses-an-indexer-change-with-the-old-indexer", :kind :function, :intent "bin/seon check rehearses publication and adoption of the candidate in the RUNNING JVM, whose compiled indexer is the old one, so it refuses any change to what the indexer reads: lane task-tags' annotation change (:seon/tasks handles to quoted symbols) failed with `seon.fn/task-links` refusing its result (data/clusters/default/logs/operator-check-20261007T145415.659.edn), while the same candidate booted green in the throwaway JVM. The throwaway half never runs because the rehearsal refused first. Done when check rehearses an indexer change in the throwaway JVM against a copy or branch of the live store (new code adopting the live data), and an annotation-format change passes check.", :must-read #{seon.operator/check!}, :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/task-tags/task-tags-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "build-cache-keys-hash-printed-text"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Two build-cache keys still hash printed text, not structure: `seon.dev.dependency-digest/digest-declarations!` feeds pr-str of deps.edn's declarations (under fixed print bindings) into SHA-256, and `seon.operator/program-classes` hashes pr-str of [trees cache-identity]. `seon.id/id` hashes structure and loads in babashka (verified 2026-10-07: same ids in bb and the JVM); dependency_digest.clj is also loaded by path on tools.deps' tool classpath, which does not hold src/. Done when both keys hash their data with `seon.id/id` (or the source bytes they derive from) and no build-cache key prints a value to hash it."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/program-classes}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/task-tags/task-tags-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name
            "a-candidate-that-babashka-cannot-load-passes-the-check",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "The operator (`bin/seon`, `script/seon/operator.clj`) runs under babashka and requires first-party namespaces such as `seon.error.refusal`, but nothing proves a candidate's operator closure loads in babashka: `bin/seon check` runs HEAD's operator and boots the candidate in a JVM. A JVM-only form (lane refusal-walk-fix used `(.hasheq ^clojure.lang.IHashEq x)` in `seon.error.refusal/referenced`) passed lint and the JVM tests while `bb --config /dev/null -cp script:src:resources -e \"(require 'seon.operator)\"` failed with `Unable to resolve classname: clojure.lang.IHashEq`; committing it would break every bin/seon command. Correct: the check (or one test beside `seon.cluster.subprocess-test/plain-babashka-cannot-load-the-quarry`) requires `seon.operator` in babashka from the candidate's archive and refuses when it fails. Proof test: babashka loads the candidate's operator."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-command-with-help-answers-an-error-map-instead-of-its-usage"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`bin/seon check --help` (and any command with --help) answers a full error map, \"Unknown option.\" with its chain, layer and timestamps, instead of the command's usage line (2026-10-08, lane impl-ref1). The usage exists: `bin/seon help` prints every command's flags. An agent's first natural probe of a command costs it an error to read. Done when `--help` after any command prints that command's usage from the same usage table and exits 0, as a regression on `seon.operator/parse-argv`."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/parse-argv}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-restart-prunes-the-program-classes-a-starting-check-uses"
             :kind :function
             :intent "On 2026-10-09 a `bin/seon start --head` that filled classes for a changed dependency (reference-code/sci) pruned a program-classes directory (target/dev-program-classes/8c25a8da…) while a `bin/seon check` was starting from it; the check's throwaway JVM failed with NoSuchFileException on instaparse/gll$sub_sequence.class and refused the lane commit (retry passed). The prune must never delete classes a starting or live check holds (`seon.operator/prune-program-classes!` and the hold markers of LR-SHARED)."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/check!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "one-test-check-takes-74-seconds"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.operator/check!` (`bin/seon check --test NS/NAME`) took 74-95 s per run in fan-out 9 (2026-10-09, 995b01c2d) for a handful of named tests whose bodies run in milliseconds; a throwaway JVM is ready in about 10 s with cached classes (`bin/seon start --head` ready-ms 10158). The cost must follow the tests requested: a check of one test costs JVM readiness plus loading the namespaces that test reaches plus the test, not whole-program publication, adoption rehearsal, full lint or whole-program load. Measure each phase of `seon.operator/check!` first (lane platform-step-card, docs/seon/schedule/cards/PLATFORM-STEP.md), then remove or narrow every phase not proportional to the requested tests. The same path serves the platform test step (an agent's host-bound change runs its affected tests in a throwaway JVM), so its floor sets how fast platform work can iterate."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/check! seon.operator/operator-check-bound-ms}}
 #:seon.task{:name "check-classpath-build-races-in-tools-deps"
             :kind :function
             :intent "bin/seon check sometimes fails at `clojure -Spath -M:dev:test` with 'Error building classpath. class java.util.HashMap$Node cannot be cast to class java.util.HashMap$TreeNode' from `org.apache.maven.model.validation.DefaultModelValidator/validateId` under `clojure.tools.deps/expand-deps` (a HashMap shared across its parallel children tasks); the identical retry passes. Seen twice on 2026-10-10 (lane loop-impl-cost; operator-check-20261010T094742.914, operator-check-20261010T095938.248). Done when the check's classpath step cannot fail this way (a cached classpath per deps digest, or a serialized expansion), with the retry no longer needed."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/request!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "bin-lane-claim-refuses-the-lanes-own-edit-and-any-new-file"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`bin/lane claim` refuses a path the same lane edited before claiming it, and any untracked new file, as someone else's uncommitted change (loop-prove and loop-impl-flow, 86b7cdbbe and aca57cc39, 2026-10-10), so lanes moved files aside, claimed, and moved them back. The rule that protects another lane's work in progress (claim refuses a dirty unclaimed path, bin/lane header lines 52-54) has no way to tell a lane's own edit from a foreign one. Done when claim accepts a dirty path whose only writer is the claiming lane (its released.holders record, or the dirty digest it last saw) and an untracked file no other lane holds, still refusing a path another lane claimed or edited, with a regression in test/seon/operator_test.clj style on a scratch repository."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/check!}
             :evidence #{"bin/lane"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "check-test-var-alone-runs-against-head-and-a-new-test-is-not-eligible"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`bin/seon check --test VAR` with no candidate paths runs against HEAD, so a test the working tree adds is answered \"The requested test is not eligible.\" (`seon.test/select`) unless the candidate paths are also given (loop-prove, 86b7cdbbe, 2026-10-10). Done when a named test that exists only in the working tree's changed files is either run (the check derives its candidate from the tree's dirty paths) or refused with the one fix (\"pass the file that declares it\"), asserted on the check's answer. `bin/lane commit` already passes its PATHS and is unaffected."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/check! seon.test/select}
             :evidence #{"bin/seon"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "bin-lane-commit-cannot-accept-a-test-already-red-on-head"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`bin/lane commit LANE --test ... PATHS` (the throwaway-check path) has no way to accept a test that is already red on HEAD, so lanes name vars around the red test (loop-prove, 86b7cdbbe, 2026-10-10; the branch-evidence path has `--accept-red-before TEST`). Done when the check path takes the same flag, records that the named test was red on the parent commit (the parent's stored run) and lets only that test stay red, with the commit trailer naming it; any other red still refuses."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/check! seon.cluster.branch-evidence/branch-evidence}
             :evidence #{"bin/lane"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-extracted-archive-is-read-only"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A committed archive `data/source/<sha>` (`seon.operator/committed-source!`) is the running JVM's program and working directory, yet an agent's relative `(spit \"src/seon/sci/forgive.clj\" ...)` rewrote the running archive's platform source (observed 2026-10-10, chain run 2), to be loaded by the next reload or by a restart reusing the sha. Done when `seon.operator/seal-archive!` clears the write bits of every committed regular file of the archive before it is moved into place, deleters (`prune-archives!`, nuke) still delete it whole, and a committed source file refuses a write. The same holds for the gitlink pins the archive links (`reference-code/*` resolves to `data/source/pins/...`, which a JVM loads Datahike, SCI and Malli from): `seon.operator/seal-pinned!` seals the files git tracks at each pin (nested gitlinks included) after tools.deps preparation, leaving untracked build products writable, and pruning still deletes pins whole."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/committed-source! seon.operator/seal-archive! seon.operator/seal-pinned! seon.operator/prune-archives!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-booting-jvms-class-directory-is-pruned-under-it"
  :kind :function
  :intent "`bin/seon start --head` onto af605e344 (2026-10-10 17:37) failed: the new JVM died with `java.nio.file.NoSuchFileException` for `target/dev-program-classes/041748f9.../seon/program$entity_identities.class`, and the directory was gone afterwards, while lane check JVMs (`bin/seon check`) sharing the linked `target` ran concurrently. The retry booted. `seon.operator/prune-archives!` and the program-class pruning protect a class directory a live process names (`seon.operator-move-test/a-class-directory-a-live-process-names-is-never-pruned`), but a replacement JVM that has not yet advertised itself is not live. Correct: a class directory selected for a boot that has not finished is held from its selection until the boot exits or advertises; the failed start's diagnostic names the pruner. Its rollback then reported `:rollback :unknown` with no captured heads, which reads as data loss though none occurred: state that nothing was captured because the replacement died first."
  :must-read #{seon.operator/prune-archives! seon.operator/request!}
  :evidence #{"docs/seon/plan.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-jvm-runs-in-a-run-directory-not-its-archive"
  :kind :function
  :intent "Fences outside the night (no-fences law): `seon.operator/seal-archive!` (9cd874d94) and `seon.operator/seal-pinned!` (4027882d4) clear write bits on the committed archive and pinned checkouts because an agent's relative `spit` rewrote the running archive (observed), yet a sibling write plus rename still replaces a sealed file. Root design: the JVM's working directory is a per-run directory the operator creates, never the archive, and the archive and pins are read only through the classpath; a relative write then lands in the run directory. Done when both sealing functions are deleted and an agent's relative `spit` leaves the archive unchanged."
  :must-read #{seon.operator/seal-archive! seon.operator/seal-pinned!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-test-launched-jvm-exits-when-its-launcher-exits"
  :kind :function
  :intent "A JVM launched for a test or a check outlives its launcher. On 2026-10-11 root found nine JVMs still running with parent pid 1, started 2026-10-08..10: roots data/check/<sha>-<pid>/.../tmp/b1b-* (children of `bin/seon check` throwaway JVMs) and data/source/871e6a979.../tmp/b1b-* (children of default while it ran 871e6a979), each a scratch root of `seon.cluster.boot-test` drills. `seon.operator/launch-child!` starts every JVM as a detached daemon, as `start` needs for default, so when the launching test JVM is replaced, stopped or killed at its bound before the drill's cleanup runs, nothing ends the child; root had to kill them by hand. Reproduced the same day by lane revert-verify: a full compiled tier on an isolated root (tmp/test-runs/revert-baseline-*) left boot-test drill JVMs under its data/source/<sha>/tmp/b1b-* with parent pid 1 while their test members ran past the request's 240 s check limit. Done when a JVM launched under a test or check root (`seon.operator/launched-throwaway!`, the drills' `bin/seon --root ... start`) exits when its launcher process exits (the launcher's pid observed through `java.lang.ProcessHandle/onExit`, decided at launch from the request, never by a sweep), while default still outlives `bin/seon start`; proven by a drill that kills a launcher and observes its child's exit within the bound."
  :must-read #{seon.operator/launch-child! seon.operator/launched-throwaway! seon.operator/check!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-move-test-calls-launch-form-by-its-current-arity"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Central round 2026-10-12: `seon.operator-move-test/a-move-states-branch-heads-before-its-boot-writes` (script/seon/operator_move_test.clj) is red at HEAD with `Wrong number of args (3) passed to: seon.operator/launch-form`: the test calls `launch-form` with three arguments after a change of its arity (suspect 05139fa1f, the launcher pid for a throwaway JVM). Update the call to the current arity or, if the test's third argument is still wanted, restore it at the owner. Done when the test is green."
             :must-read #{seon.operator/launch-form seon.operator-move-test/a-move-states-branch-heads-before-its-boot-writes}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/central-round.md"}}]
